>We do nightly backups (5 tapes, 1 for each night of the week, on a
>rotating basis) on an Alpha 3000/M600 and a DECstation 5000/200 to a 5Gb
>backup tape device (TZ09) attached to the Alpha.  For the past 2 weeks,
>I find the tape ejected every morning with the tape at about halfway.
>At first I thought the tape is full so the drive ejects the tape.  But
>it turned out that the tape is not full; the backup just goes to a
>certain point and ejects the tape.  Data from the beinning of tape to
>point of eject are good.  Does anyone know what the problem is?  The
>Alpha data and the DECstation data are backed up as separate filesets
>with the Alpha being the first.  Could it be that when backup of Alpha
>encounters the beginning of file of DECstation from the previous week,
>backup stops rather than overwrite?  Thanks much!

I don't think the vdump or the dump (if that is what you are using) reads the 
previous data; it simply overwrites everything. One idea I have about your 
problem is that vdump keeps track of how much it has written to the device. If 
it thinks the tape is at the end, vdump simply ejects it. This can be salvaged 
by explicitly specifying the density and length of the tape on the command 
line when starting the vdump (or dump).

Hope this helps.


