> Anyone know why it is that if I create a file in /tmp (or /var/tmp)
> the file belongs to group "system" which I don't belong to?  I could
> understand it if the sgid bit was on on the directory but it isn't.
> 
> This "feature" makes it annoying when trying to look at files (eg.
> daemon logs) since the umask for our group doesn't include other
> permissions.
> 
> Is there some way to change this behaviour?
> 
> I'd also like to enable the sgid permission on directories behaviour
> that I am used to on other machines:  when creating a directory in a
> directory where the sgid bit is on the new directory also has the
> sgid bit on.  Anyone know how to do this?  The file (or directory) is
> created in the group set by the directory but the sgid bit is not
> propagated unless it is a NFS filesystem!
> 
> --
> Andrew.

Set the sys_v_mode "tunable" in  your kernel configuration file
(under /sys/conf), like in

    #
    # enable System V file/directory creation behaviour
    #

    sys_v_mode  1

and recompile your kernel (using doconfig -c YOUR_KERNEL_CONFIG_FILE).
This will cure both problems. BTW, this is vaguely mentioned
in man 2 mkdir and somewhere in the Bookreader documentation.

/Peter



