In article <320907C1.82F@chinf.mail.abb.com>, Christian Zamecnik <christian.zamecnik@chinf.mail.abb.com> writes:
>Hello out there!
>
>A friend of mine has a problem with a DEC OSF/1 station, which is
>attached to a FDDI-Ring (single attached, but dual-homed).
>
>The station is always reporting the following problem/error:
>
>30-Jul-1996 00:54:14  fta0: Ring initialized.
.
.
.

>30-Jul-1996 15:55:22  fta0: Ring initialized.
>
>First of all: what exactly does this statement "Ring initialized." mean?
>It still could be absolutely normal, though.

It is absolutely normal.  It means that some other node has entered or left
the FDDI ring.  The DUnix driver for their FDDI devices does a very good
job of reporting unusual event on the FDDI ring.

Unfortunately, the firmware/driver can sometimes get so confused by errors
on the FDDI that it will stop.  At that point the only thing you can do is
reboot.  Other than this detail, we have had no problems using FDDI.
Jack Patteeuw

