Date: 1996/08/23
Message-ID: <321E184E.167E@kmail.nasa.ksc.gov>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 176089187
cc: earl.foster@kmail.ksc.nasa.gov
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: NASA, Kennedy Space Center
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc
x-mailer: Mozilla 2.02 (X11; I; OSF1 V4.0 alpha)


We are running DEC osf1 servers (2200s) with the syslog daemon
logging messages from our applications. It is set up in the
syslog.conf file to send messages to a user id. 

A portion of syslog.conf file follows:
   
    
      local4.debug            userid1
      local4.err              userid2

This works fine ...... but ........ every once in a while
the system process table files up with syslogd daemons.
 
They just keep forking off the main until the proc table is full.
We have also found that these process' can get themselves into
an uninteruptable state (ps with a U in the state field) and the
only thing you can do is reboot!!!!!!!!!

MY QUESTIONS ARE:

  1. Why does the syslogd daemon make copies of itself?

  2. Has anybody had this problem of there being so many
     syslogd daemons created that id fills up the proc table
     and you must reboot?
     

Thanks in advance for any help ... this one has me stumped!!

earl.foster@kmail.ksc.nasa.gov



